A||N||4||Will||Yaw oscillation: bumpsteer/damping or alignment?||||1||
Z||000000||Will||06-09-2001||11:45 PM||||I just finished putting Delrin front suspension bushings and UHMW rear bushings in my car last weekend. I drove it for a week that way (had removed rear anti-sway bar for shits and giggles) and liked the new controllability.<P>Today I had the car aligned, and went to the rear bar setup I described here: <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/010981.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/010981.html</A> <BR>The alignment went from -1.5 rear camber, slight toe-out, slightly positive front camber, slight toe-out to -1.0 rear camber, -0.5 front camber, stock toe specs all around.<P><BR>I just went over a mountain, and in sweeping corners near the limit the car developed a nasty yaw oscillation which I had to be extremely smooth to prevent or correct.<P>What's happening?<P>I can think of two posibilites:<BR>1) The new sway bar setup is too stiff, making the car underdamped with worn out OEM struts. This would allow a roll oscillation, which would transfer to a yaw oscillation through rear suspension bump steer, OR<P>2) The new alignment is doing something weird.<P>Any thoughts?||||reg||1
Z||000001||Fierowrecker||06-10-2001||12:30 AM||||Or you still have rubber engine cradle bushings and engine mounts...<BR>crash...||||reg||1
Z||000002||84Bill||06-10-2001||12:37 AM||||Did you do anything to the steering damper?||||reg||3
Z||000003||Will||06-10-2001||09:28 PM||||Aluminum cradle bushings<BR>Rubber engine mounts, but rod-end dogbone.<BR>It didn't do this oscillation before I changed the bar, and those conditions have existed since before I changed the bar, so it's not those things.<P>The steering damper has been gone for months with no ill effects.<P>After driving the car some more, I think that I just don't have enough rear tire, and my traction center is too far forward.<P>Some Corvette tires ought to cure that problem.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000004||lowCG||06-10-2001||10:35 PM||||I found that the more rear toe,the less adhesion;wound up with 1/8" totall rear toe for a good balance of predictability and adhesion.You still may need close to 1.5 degrees camber in the rear also.<BR>Sounds like the front shocks might not have enough rebound control(maybe);it takes alot more up there in rear engined cars to eliminate the fluttering like you described.I'd try some soft,sticky tires like Bridgestone RE71s or Yokohama A509s if everything else is pretty cool,then see what happens.<BR>My car used to do the same thing until I stiffened up the rear spring rate to a point where it just drifts smoothly and evenly at,uh,higher speeds.||||reg||1
